00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.013
- Hey everyone.

00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:06.660
Welcome into Market Takes.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.493
You know what?

00:00:07.493 --> 00:00:08.326
I know you could have been

00:00:08.326 --> 00:00:09.159
anywhere in the world

00:00:09.159 --> 00:00:09.992
but you're here with me,

00:00:09.992 --> 00:00:11.130
and I appreciate that.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:14.220
I am Dion Rabouin, reporter
for the Wall Street Journal.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.870
Listen, it is a short week

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.580
but we are still getting lots

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.840
of data out this week.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.280
I'm gonna tell you the three

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.960
most important things you need

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.550
to look out for this week,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.810
and they are three reports

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.460
that are all coming out on Thursday.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.060
We'll be right here doing
Market Takes Thursday at noon.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.230
We usually do it at 10:00 AM,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.820
so make sure you adjust

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.130
your sleep cycle and all that

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.660
so you catch us here on
Market Takes Thursday at noon.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:37.890
But we're gonna be talking through,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.020
today, the Existing Home Sales Report

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.220
that comes out on Thursday,

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:43.140
the Leading Economic Indicators Report

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.310
that comes out on Thursday,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.770
and the Initial Jobless Claims Report

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.540
that also comes out on Thursday.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.070
Thursday is gonna be a big day,

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:50.903
I'm gonna tell you why.

00:00:50.903 --> 00:00:52.080
So first things first,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.150
let's talk about that
Existing Home Sales Report.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:57.210
That is the main report
on the housing market

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.830
because most homes that are sold

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.570
in the United States are existing homes,

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:01.980
as in already built homes,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:02.820
not new homes.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.050
One that you buy that was built

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.200
in the 50s, 60s, 70s, 80s, etc, etc.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.070
That's the big one,

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.510
and that shows us what's happening

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.550
with about 80% of the housing market.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.560
But what we have seen recently is

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.300
some data on those new home sales

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.340
that suggest that maybe that's part

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.420
of the market is heating up,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.760
and the existing home sales
market could be heating

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.593
up as well.

00:01:21.593 --> 00:01:23.340
So far this week, we have already gotten

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.770
the NAHB Housing Market Index,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.447
as well the Home...

00:01:27.447 --> 00:01:30.330
New Home Starts, or Housing Starts.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.130
That data came out on Monday.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.900
And just this morning on Tuesday,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.250
we have seen a big pickup.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.083
First things first,

00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:37.590
I wanna throw up here on your screen

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.540
the NAHB Housing Market Index.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.060
Now, this data shows a very clear trend

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.320
and this is what we're seeing

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.120
across the new homes part

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.200
of the housing market.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.990
Very clearly, home sales
declining, declining, declining,

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.580
and that's really market sentiment.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.110
That's how folks feel about

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:52.943
the housing market.

00:01:52.943 --> 00:01:55.710
When we talk about the
Existing Home Sales Report...

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:56.543
Or sorry, when we talk about

00:01:56.543 --> 00:01:57.870
the Housing Market Report,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.360
that's a survey of
about 900 home builders.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.950
A reading above 50 indicates

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.630
a favorable outlook on home sales,

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.700
below 50 indicates a negative outlook.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.080
And as you see up there on your screen,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.240
we had fallen well below 50,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.100
hitting almost that 30 level,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.420
at the end of last year.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.650
And then starting this year,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.390
you see month over month,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.740
every month picking up

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:17.670
and rising higher.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.230
And so, just last month we got...

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.063
Or I'm sorry, yeah,

00:02:20.063 --> 00:02:21.120
just last month we got a reading

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.580
of 55 on that outlook.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.040
And so, that just
illustrating very clearly

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.350
that confidence, optimism,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.420
the outlook on the housing market is

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.440
really picking up.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.760
Those home builders are going out,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.110
building more homes,

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.700
putting more inventory out there.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.560
That should provide more supply.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.630
And as we know, there's a lot of demand

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:40.980
and supply is rising,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:41.813
that could do something

00:02:41.813 --> 00:02:42.930
to bring down houses...

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.880
Or to bring down prices, excuse me,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.200
in the overall housing market.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.430
But again, we have not

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.320
really seen that same pickup,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.640
and we need to see a decline

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.320
in the number of existing homes sold

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.550
to bring down those prices.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.590
So a very clear trend when we see

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.320
what's going on with
the Home Builder Index,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.430
with new home sales.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:01.590
New home sales actually were the highest

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.267
since May of 2022.

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:06.060
We also saw the NAHB Housing Index,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.677
that was the highest since July of 2022.

00:03:08.677 --> 00:03:10.140
And the new home sales...

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.670
I'm sorry, housing starts was

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.040
the highest since May of 2022.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.200
And we saw those new home sales rise

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.520
in a big way to the highest

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.170
since May of 2022 as well.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.430
So housing starts,

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.027
which is the forward-looking indicator,

00:03:22.027 --> 00:03:24.127
"how many homes were actually built?

00:03:24.127 --> 00:03:25.477
"Housing construction, or how many

00:03:25.477 --> 00:03:28.350
"new residential permits
started on being built?"

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.510
That was the highest since May of 2022.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.760
The NAHB Housing Market Index,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.250
showing home builder
confidence, sentiment, all that,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:37.050
highest since July of 2022.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.300
And the new home sales was the highest

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.500
since May of 2022,

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.990
with 683,000 units sold.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.130
This really just showing us

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.690
that the housing market is booming

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.220
and coming back in a big way

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.480
on the new home side.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.770
But as I said earlier,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.900
existing home sales are what really matter

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.300
and we will get that report on Thursday.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.290
And we're gonna find out if

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.660
the existing home sales
trend has continued

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.950
along with the new home sales trend.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.300
So let's throw up here a chart

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.190
on your screen of the existing home sales

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.650
and what we've seen from that report.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:05.760
As you see there,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.140
new home sales have been declining

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.790
in that same way over the past year

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:09.900
into the end of the year.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.140
And then, in January we got a big pickup

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.220
in the number of new...

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.770
In the number of existing
home sales sold, excuse me,

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:18.000
and that showed...

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.320
And that has actually been declining.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.450
So if we see that trend continue,

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.730
that could put downward pressure on prices

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:25.140
and that could be a good thing

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.070
for the housing market.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.630
Now, that chart up there on your screen,

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:29.417
just illustrating that we have seen

00:04:29.417 --> 00:04:31.620
a turn down in existing home sales,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.560
but still above the level and the trend

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:35.550
that we saw coming into

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:36.600
the end of last year.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:37.433
Why does that matter

00:04:37.433 --> 00:04:38.880
for the everyday average person?

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.969
Well, if home sales
prices are coming down,

00:04:41.969 --> 00:04:43.860
that means that rents can come down

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.270
because those landlords that are buying

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.740
those houses and renting 'em out

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.630
to people aren't gonna be paying as much,

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:49.620
they're not gonna need to make

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.020
as much in rent to justify that purchase.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.070
That's gonna allow them

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:54.120
to give some flexibility

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.110
to their tenants,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.090
going with the overall economy.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.010
But if those prices keep rising,

00:04:59.010 --> 00:04:59.843
keep getting higher,

00:04:59.843 --> 00:05:01.710
as we've seen with the
new home sales market,

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.930
that could put upward
pressure on inflation,

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:05.400
push inflation back up.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.480
That gets to the Fed,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.040
that gets to asset prices,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:08.910
all of those things,

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.440
so that would be an overall negative

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.273
for the market.

00:05:11.273 --> 00:05:12.150
So you're gonna wanna watch

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.350
that Existing Home Sales Report

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.180
that we get out on Thursday.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.370
We're expecting to see
another decline in May,

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:18.840
just like we saw in previous months,

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.180
in April and in March before that.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.260
But if we don't get that

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.850
and we get an unexpected pickup,

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.860
that could be bad news for the market

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:26.970
so you'll wanna watch out for that.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:27.990
Next thing I wanna talk about is

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.190
the leading index...

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.500
Or the Leading Economic Indicators Index.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.060
I wanna put a chart up here on your screen

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.140
to show you what we're seeing

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:35.580
from that report as well.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.620
Now, this report, much less bullish.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.630
There's really not a lot to it.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:40.463
As you see there,

00:05:40.463 --> 00:05:42.327
when the index starts falling below...

00:05:42.327 --> 00:05:44.460
When the Index of Leading
Economic Indicators falls

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.530
below zero, we tend to have recessions.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.080
You see that when it holds
below that consistently,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.640
recessions tend to follow it.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.590
And we saw it in 2008,

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:53.910
we saw it in 2020,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.410
and it looks like right now

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:56.400
we are seeing that same trend,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.780
and actually a more clear

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.220
and long-term trend

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.470
in that Leading Economic
Indicators Report.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.390
So that chart is illustrating just

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.130
the clear trend that we are in right now

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.390
with those numbers.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.250
May expected to show another decline,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:09.630
actually a bigger decline,

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.027
than what we saw in April.

00:06:11.027 --> 00:06:13.710
In April, the Leading Economic
Indicators Report declined

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:15.570
for the 13th straight month.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.160
So we've seen more than a year

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.980
of continued decline on that report,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.170
and actually it is a
steeper rate of decline

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:23.610
for the previous six months

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.050
than the six months before that.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.880
So really just showing right now,

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:27.713
things are not good.

00:06:27.713 --> 00:06:29.940
The LEI is the conference forward index.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.100
It usually is a good predictor of GDP.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.870
GDP is how we measure recession.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.310
So when it goes down,

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.480
generally we tend to be

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.070
in recessionary territory.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.870
Includes things like the S&amp;P 500,

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.970
the treasury yield curve, credit indexes,

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:42.930
but also a lot of things

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.600
like manufacturing data, business surveys,

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.000
like the ISM and Purchasing
Managers Indexes.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.070
So we know the S&amp;P is really picked up,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.260
we know that stocks have
been booming lately,

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.880
that could push that index back

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.170
into positive territory.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.003
Definitely will be something

00:06:56.003 --> 00:06:57.180
you wanna look out for.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:01.020
The Atlanta Fed's GDP now
reports showing expectations

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:01.853
for the Fed...

00:07:01.853 --> 00:07:03.780
Or for the GDP to come in positive,

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:05.520
almost right at two percent.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.410
The Federal Reserve is expecting GDP

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:08.370
to recover this year

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.080
and stay outta that negative territory,

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.310
so we shall see.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.300
This will be a good report

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.740
that you'll wanna watch on Thursday

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:14.580
about what happens.

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.840
Last thing we wanna talk about is

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.700
those initial jobless claims number.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:18.600
Let's put this chart up here

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.940
on your screen of initial jobless claims

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:21.773
and look at that.

00:07:21.773 --> 00:07:22.606
You see there,

00:07:22.606 --> 00:07:24.810
we are seeing a clear trend of picking up.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:25.920
But most importantly,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.020
as you see the far right of your screen,

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.960
those last two numbers have been

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.080
the highest we've seen since January 2022,

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.880
so well above the trend.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.190
And we don't know if those are outliers.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:39.630
Are those something like what we saw

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.220
a few weeks ago, or a couple months ago,

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.200
with Massachusetts having some fraud

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.220
in its claims?

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.110
Are those, though, the start of a trend?

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:49.020
And if they are, could
we be seeing big pickups

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.780
in unemployment and layoffs?

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.130
And could that be a sign that

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.020
the job market is starting to weaken,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:56.340
the labor market is starting to weaken?

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.770
So keep your eyes on on this report,

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.480
the US Initial Jobless Claims.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:01.800
That's how many people are applying

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.660
for unemployment insurance

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:04.493
and things like that

00:08:04.493 --> 00:08:05.490
because they've been laid off.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:07.200
This chart illustrating that we have seen

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.460
a very clear pickup over

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.290
the last two weeks in that data.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:11.123
And we will send...

00:08:11.123 --> 00:08:12.600
We will look and continue to see

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:13.620
if that trend continues.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.540
So we're gonna break all that down here

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.610
on Thursday at noon on Market Takes.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:18.600
I hope you'll be with us,

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:19.740
I hope you'll be with me,

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.330
as you are today.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:22.163
Thank you so much.

00:08:22.163 --> 00:08:24.240
I'm Dion Rabouin, reporter
with the Wall Street Journal,

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:25.830
and we will see you right back here,

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.450
Thursday at noon, on Market Takes.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:28.353
See you next time.

